{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784646303,"nonce":"PekfeHYsTzMjxt41vNNuxjjIsXA=","message":"Root-cause analysis (no fix proposed).\n\nThe default take-method is chosen from the **patch count alone**, without\nconsulting whether a cover letter exists.\n\n`TakeScreen` sets `self._default_method = default_method or ('linear' if\nnum_patches == 1 else 'merge')` (`src/b4/review_tui/_modals.py:750-751`). So\nany multi-patch series defaults to merge, even when the submitter sent no cover\nletter.\n\nWhen there is no cover, the merge commit body falls back to a placeholder: the\nmessage construction (`src/b4/review_tui/_tracking_app.py:2720-2728`) sets\n`covermessage = '(no cover letter message)'` when the cover text is empty, and\nthat is substituted into `DEFAULT_MERGE_TEMPLATE` (`src/b4/mbox.py:31-38`) --\nhence the \"weird\" merge commit Mark describes.\n\nCover-letter absence *is* detectable (`b4.review._review` synthesizes the\nmarker \"NOTE: No cover letter provided by the author.\"; `_review_app.py:287`\ncomputes `_has_cover` from it), but nothing feeds that into the default-method\nchoice, and it is not surfaced in the series list or the take dialog\n(`_pw_app.py` series label, `_modals.py` TakeScreen) -- so the user gets no\nsignal that the cover is missing before choosing merge.","files":null}]}